content:
Convert the card below. The JSON `card` holds the situation text, the options (already renumbered O1, O2, ...), the
considerations (already renumbered C1, C2, ...; each has the single `fact` sentence it was written around), the flip
conditions (already numbered flip1.., noflip1.., watch1..), and the original `judgment` prose, which refers to options by
their old letters and to considerations by their old ids (c1, c2, ...). `letter_to_option` and `old_to_new` give the renaming.

Return a JSON object with exactly these keys.

1. `facts`: the list of facts the decider knew at the decision point, taken ONLY from the situation text. One short,
   self-contained sentence each (copy numbers, times and names exactly; do not merge unrelated things). Typically 6 to 12
   facts, enough that every consideration's `fact` sentence is covered. Each item:
   `{"id": "F1", "kind": ..., "status": ..., "fact": "..."}`, ids F1, F2, ... in the order the situation tells them.
   `kind` is one of: `situation` (what is happening), `capability` (what the agent or others can do or have), `authority`
   (rules, orders, delegations, who owns the call), `intelligence` (what is assessed or inferred about others' intent
   or knowledge). `status` is `confirmed` (observed or stated as certain) or `assessed` (an estimate, inference or judgment).
2. `consideration_facts`: for each consideration id (C1, C2, ...) the list of fact ids it rests on (1 to 4 ids, all from
   your `facts`). Start from the consideration's own `fact` sentence and attach every fact the sentence relies on; a fact
   may support several considerations; every fact should support at least one consideration where the card allows it.
3. `reasoning`: rewrite the card's `judgment` into these fields, same content and voice, shortened only where it repeats:
   - `read`: what is really going on, and what a less experienced decider would miss.
   - `weighing`: which interest gave way to which, and the conditions under which that holds.
   - `tradecraft`: how a seasoned practitioner carries the choice out (decision practice only).
   - `uncertainty`: what was not known and why the choice holds anyway.
   - `handoff`: what the decider settles on its own authority and what goes to whom. If the card has no `handoff` text, write
     one from the card's other text and add the note "handoff written by converter" to `caveats`.
   - `tempting_wrong`: `{"pull": "...", "why_wrong": "..."}` (the option is set by the converter).
   - `rejected`: an object mapping option ids (O1, ...) to one line each, for the options the card gives a reason for
     (empty object if the card has none).
   In this prose write options as `{O2}`, flip conditions as `{flip1}`, considerations as `{C1}` and facts as `{F3}`
   (curly braces, exactly this form), never the old letters or old ids. Only use ids that exist. Do not write a bare "option B".
4. `caveats`: a list of short strings naming anything you wrote that the card does not say (an inferred fact, a rephrased
   estimate, a written handoff). Empty list if none.

Card (JSON):
{
 "card": {
  "setting": "2028 · division intelligence section, force-protection threat analysis at an Army post",
  "decider": "the agent, under the section's SOP; the oversight officer and counsel own oversight rulings",
  "agent": "the section's analysis-workflow agent: files material, drafts threat products, routes by SOP",
  "agent_role": "execute",
  "situation": "At 0940 two analysts searching open websites for threats to Fort Garrow flag posts by the Tri-County Defense Force, a self-described militia in the surrounding county. Its leader vows to \"drive the division off this land by force,\" and one post shows photos of the main gate. The analysts estimate about 40 members. Nothing in the posts or holdings ties the group to a foreign power or international terrorism; its members appear to be U.S. persons. The section SOP, under AR 381-10 and DoD Manual 5240.01, admits U.S.-person information to intelligence files only within an assigned mission (foreign intelligence and counterintelligence) and gives domestic threats to the post to the Provost Marshal, who works them with CID. This week's threat assessment closes at 1500 and goes to the commanding general and 31 units at 1600. The intelligence oversight officer is on duty; the SOP sends unclear oversight questions to her and counsel.",
  "question": "What should the agent do with the militia posts the analysts flagged?",
  "options": {
   "O1": {
    "do": "enter the militia posts in the intelligence database and report them in this week's threat assessment",
    "move": "act",
    "irreversible": true
   },
   "O2": {
    "do": "refer the militia posts to the Provost Marshal's office and keep them out of all intelligence files",
    "move": "act"
   },
   "O3": {
    "do": "hold the militia posts back and ask the intelligence oversight officer to rule on them before any filing",
    "move": "ask",
    "to": "the intelligence oversight officer"
   }
  },
  "considerations": [
   {
    "id": "C1",
    "was": "c1",
    "fact": "the posts are public, found by analysts searching open websites",
    "equity": "usp_privacy",
    "favors": [
     "O1"
    ],
    "weight": "weak",
    "why": "public words seem to carry no privacy cost"
   },
   {
    "id": "C2",
    "was": "c2",
    "fact": "a militia of about 40 vows to drive the division out by force and posts photos of the main gate",
    "equity": "own_force_life",
    "favors": [
     "O1",
     "O2"
    ],
    "weight": "strong",
    "why": "a threat to people on the post must reach those who can act on it, but a vow with no date or plan can wait hours"
   },
   {
    "id": "C3",
    "was": "c3",
    "fact": "this week's threat assessment closes at 1500 and goes to the commanding general and 31 units",
    "equity": "own_force_life",
    "favors": [
     "O1"
    ],
    "weight": "strong",
    "why": "the assessment would put the warning in front of every unit this afternoon"
   },
   {
    "id": "C4",
    "was": "c4",
    "fact": "nothing ties the group to a foreign power or international terrorism; its members appear to be U.S. persons",
    "equity": "lawful_authority",
    "favors": [
     "O2"
    ],
    "weight": "strong",
    "why": "with no foreign tie this looks like a domestic threat, law enforcement's to work"
   },
   {
    "id": "C5",
    "was": "c5",
    "fact": "the SOP admits U.S.-person information only within an assigned mission and gives domestic threats to the Provost Marshal",
    "equity": "lawful_authority",
    "favors": [
     "O2"
    ],
    "weight": "bar",
    "why": "a domestic group lies outside the mission, so intelligence files and products on it are barred"
   },
   {
    "id": "C6",
    "was": "c6",
    "fact": "the intelligence oversight officer is on duty, and the SOP sends unclear oversight questions to her",
    "equity": "chain_of_command",
    "favors": [
     "O3"
    ],
    "weight": "strong",
    "why": "U.S.-person material touching force protection waits for her ruling before it is filed or routed"
   }
  ],
  "verdict": {
   "choice": "O3",
   "basis": "recommended",
   "decisive": [
    "C5",
    "C6"
   ]
  },
  "flip_conditions": {
   "would_change": [
    {
     "id": "flip1",
     "if": "the threat is imminent, with a date or a plan",
     "then": "O2",
     "why": "the warning cannot wait for a ruling; refer it at once"
    },
    {
     "id": "flip2",
     "if": "the oversight officer has already ruled that this group's material goes to law enforcement",
     "then": "O2",
     "why": "the ruling the hold waits for already exists"
    }
   ],
   "would_not_change": [
    {
     "id": "noflip1",
     "if": "the material is wholly open source: the group's own public video, carried by local news",
     "why": "public material about U.S. persons still waits for the ruling"
    },
    {
     "id": "noflip2",
     "if": "the commander has stressed force protection",
     "why": "a ruling costs hours; emphasis does not shorten the hold"
    }
   ],
   "watch_for": [
    {
     "id": "watch1",
     "what": "the group already appears in an intelligence product or plan annex",
     "favors": [
      "O3"
     ],
     "why": "that may be a questionable intelligence activity, which goes to the oversight officer"
    }
   ]
  },
  "judgment": {
   "read": "It looks like a plain domestic referral. It is U.S.-person material about to leave the section on the agent's own reading of the SOP. The cue a novice misses: who reads the rule matters as much as the rule.",
   "weighing": "Speed of routing gives way to an oversight ruling first. A wrong routing of U.S.-person information is borne by people outside the section and is hard to recall, while a ruling costs hours. That holds while the officer is on duty and the threat names no date or plan; an imminent threat goes to law enforcement at once.",
   "tradecraft": "Park the item as held; file and send nothing. Put the posts, the gate photos and the empty search to the oversight officer with a request for a ruling today, copying counsel. Tell the analysts and operations chief why, and log it.",
   "uncertainty": "The group's size and intent are estimates. The hold costs a few hours at most; it fails only if the threat turns imminent before she rules.",
   "handoff": "The agent parks the item and frames the question. The oversight officer and counsel rule on where U.S.-person material goes; the agent carries out the ruling.",
   "tempting_wrong": {
    "option": "O2",
    "pull": "The SOP's domestic rule seems to answer it, and the Provost Marshal should hear today.",
    "why_wrong": "Under this weighing the agent does not route U.S.-person material on its own reading; a ruling costs hours and nothing is imminent."
   },
   "rejected": {
    "O1": "files and reports U.S.-person material outside the mission, with no ruling"
   }
  },
  "letter_to_option": {
   "A": "O1",
   "B": "O2",
   "C": "O3"
  },
  "old_to_new": {
   "c1": "C1",
   "c2": "C2",
   "c3": "C3",
   "c4": "C4",
   "c5": "C5",
   "c6": "C6"
  }
 }
}
